---
import Shell from '../../layouts/Shell.astro';
import Field from '../../components/Field.astro';
import { link } from '../../lib/links';
---

<Shell
  property="labs"
  title="Guild Labs — A smaller footprint. A larger possibility."
  description="Research interests in efficient language models, MoE architectures, ternary representations, and local inference. Eon is a proposed model family; a grant decision is pending."
>
  <section class="hero wrap">
    <div class="hero-copy">
      <div class="eyebrow"><span class="dot"></span> Guild Labs / research initiative</div><h1>
        A smaller footprint.<br /><em>A larger possibility.</em>
      </h1><p class="lead">
        How much intelligence can we build with less? Guild Labs explores the relationship between
        model architecture, numerical representation, and the machines that run them.
      </p><div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href="#research">Explore the research <span aria-hidden="true">↓</span></a
        >
      </div>
    </div><Field variant="labs" />
  </section>
  <section id="research" class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-label">01 / Open questions</span><h2>
        Efficiency, from the<br />model to the machine.
      </h2>
    </div><div class="pillars">
      <article class="pillar">
        <span class="number">01</span><h3>Sparse by design</h3><p>
          How can Mixture-of-Experts architectures increase model capacity while limiting the work
          required for each token?
        </p>
      </article><article class="pillar">
        <span class="number">02</span><h3>Less precision.<br />More intention.</h3><p>
          What can ternary and low-bit representations offer when we consider model quality,
          training, and hardware execution together?
        </p>
      </article><article class="pillar">
        <span class="number">03</span><h3>Local, by nature</h3><p>
          How should model design change when GPU memory is limited, system RAM is plentiful, and
          data stays on the user’s machine?
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="statement">
      <div>
        <span class="eyebrow">02 / Proposed model family</span><h2>
          Introducing an ambition.<br /><em>Eon.</em>
        </h2>
      </div><div>
        <p>
          Eon is the name of our proposed model family. Our interests include efficient large
          language models, sparse MoE architectures, and ternary representations.
        </p><p>
          The architecture, scale, and release schedule are not finalized. There are no released Eon
          checkpoints or published Eon research results to announce.
        </p><div class="notice">
          <strong>Research grant decision pending.</strong> We are awaiting a decision; funding has not
          been announced as awarded.
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <span class="section-label">03 / Representation study</span><div>
        <h2>What does a bit buy?</h2><p>
          A simple way to explore weight storage. These are mathematical estimates for hypothetical
          parameter counts, not Eon specifications or runtime memory requirements.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div><div class="paper">
      <label for="scale"
        >Hypothetical parameter count: <output id="scale-label">35</output> billion</label
      ><input
        id="scale"
        type="range"
        min="1"
        max="200"
        value="35"
        step="1"
        style="width:100%;margin:25px 0;accent-color:var(--accent)"
      /><div class="weight-bars">
        {
          [
            ['16-bit', 16],
            ['8-bit', 8],
            ['4-bit', 4],
            ['Ternary', Math.log2(3)],
          ].map(([label, bits]) => (
            <div class="weight-row">
              <>
                <span>{label}</span>
                <div class="weight-track">
                  <div class="weight-fill" style={`width:${(Number(bits) / 16) * 100}%`} />
                </div>
                <output data-bits={bits}>
                  {((35e9 * Number(bits)) / 8 / 2 ** 30).toFixed(2)} GiB
                </output>
              </>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div><p class="small-note">
        Storage = parameters × bits ÷ 8 ÷ 2³⁰. Ternary uses the ideal log₂(3) ≈ 1.585-bit
        information bound. Packing, scales, mixed precision, activations, KV cache, and runtime
        workspaces add overhead. Storage reduction alone says nothing about quality or speed.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
  <section class="section wrap">
    <div class="band">
      <div>
        <h2>Our systems work is open.</h2><p>
          Guild is where we develop and measure local inference infrastructure today.
        </p>
      </div><a class="button" href={link('guild')}
        >Explore Guild <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a
      >
    </div>
  </section>
  <script>
    const slider = document.querySelector<HTMLInputElement>('#scale');
    slider?.addEventListener('input', () => {
      document.querySelector('#scale-label')!.textContent = slider.value;
      document.querySelectorAll<HTMLOutputElement>('[data-bits]').forEach((out) => {
        out.textContent = `${((Number(slider.value) * 1e9 * Number(out.dataset.bits)) / 8 / 2 ** 30).toFixed(2)} GiB`;
      });
    });
  </script>
</Shell>
